::JOUR_U03_L07_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.COM.01] Source: The student newsroom will give a one-minute morning announcement about the school library staying open until 5:00 p.m. on Tuesdays and Thursdays during a four-week pilot program. The librarian says the schedule gives students a quiet place to finish homework after clubs and tutoring. Students must sign in at the front desk, and activity buses leave at 5:10 p.m. The principal says sign-in numbers will be reviewed before the school decides whether to continue the schedule. Question 1: Which opening best fits an oral briefing? {
~A. Read every source note in one crowded sentence.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
~B. Add opinion so the announcement sounds more exciting.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
=C. Present the verified main news first, add brief attributed details, and close with a clear listener action.#Correct. Step 1: identify the Unit 3 journalism skill in the standard. Step 2: compare the answer with the embedded source and lesson model. This answer works because it protects accuracy, audience clarity, organization, and publication readiness.
~D. Change the schedule to make the message shorter.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
}
